  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nPotential for patchy frost both Monday and Tuesday night across\nthe north.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nStrong Canadian high pressure builds into the region Monday \nnight. With mostly clear skies amd light winds during the \novernight hours, radiational cooling conditions are possible in\nthe north. This could will result in patchy frost. The frost\nwill be limited to the north due to ample cloud cover elsewhere,\nwith best conditions in the low lying cold valleys. \n\nHigh pressure will be ridged across the region Tuesday night with \nclear skies and light winds. This will provide favorable conditions \nfor radiational cooling. Patchy/areas of frost are expected Tuesday \nnight across northern and central portions of the region. \nTemperatures could fall below freezing across the normally \ncolder northern low lying and valley locations.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nA coastal low could impact at least Downeast Maine late this\nweek.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nAn ocean low will develop offshore from the Mid-Atlantic region\nmid week. At the same time, high pressure will remain centered \nnorthwest of the region. The track of the ocean low through late\nweek is still uncertain. With blocking high pressure to the \nnorthwest, uncertainty still exists regarding the northern edge \nof the precipitation shield and whether rain will reach the \nforecast area.",
